## Recent Advances in AI and Computational Oncology                                                                  
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
The integration of artificial intelligence and computational methods into oncology has accelerated across imaging,   
genomics, and therapeutic discovery. Recent studies demonstrate significant progress in early detection and          
personalized treatment strategies.                                                                                   
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
In medical imaging, deep learning models are enhancing diagnostic accuracy for various malignancies. Foundation      
models pretrained for breast cancer tasks are now being leveraged for opportunistic cardiovascular risk prediction   
from mammograms[^2609.19385]. Lightweight CNN-Transformer architectures achieve near-perfect accuracy in breast      
cancer mammography classification while maintaining clinical interpretability[^2609.18212]. Similarly, unified       
vision-language models are streamlining prostate cancer management by simultaneously generating reports, answering   
visual questions, and segmenting lesions in PSMA PET/CT scans[^2609.15603]. Autonomous robotic systems guided by     
occupancy networks are also advancing, enabling margin-negative tumor resections in partial nephrectomy              
phantoms[^2609.16186].                                                                                               
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
Computational pathology and multi-omics are revealing novel biomarkers and tumor microenvironments. Single-cell and  
spatial multi-omics have uncovered confined tumor microenvironments in ccRCC lung                                    
metastases[^10.5281_zenodo.18767278][^10.5281_zenodo.18767279]. Pan-cancer metastatic brain tumor atlases are being  
constructed to map cellular                                                                                          
heterogeneity[^10.5281_zenodo.21816284][^10.5281_zenodo.21816285][^10.5281_zenodo.21782885][^10.5281_zenodo.21782884]
. Liquid biopsy techniques utilizing cell-free DNA nucleosome positioning signatures show robust potential for       
non-invasive early cancer detection and patient stratification[^10.5526_err-00042900]. Furthermore, semi-supervised  
learning frameworks are successfully identifying prognostic biomarkers for hepatocellular carcinoma across multiple  
disease stages[^2609.17100][^2609.15638].                                                                            
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
Therapeutic discovery and drug repurposing are also benefiting from AI-driven hypothesis generation. Genetic         
algorithms coordinating multi-agent LLMs have demonstrated superior performance in identifying drug repurposing      
candidates across 34 cancer types[^2609.15938]. Metabolic interventions, such as omega-3 fatty acids, are shown to   
induce mitochondrial succinylation that triggers growth inhibition and ferroptosis in prostate cancer                
cells[^10.17638_03195029]. Additionally, fasting-mimicking diets combined with polyunsaturated fatty acids mediate   
antitumor effects in breast cancer by inducing lipid peroxidation[^10.21954_ou.ro.00108036].                         
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
Finally, standardized data sharing and mobile imaging infrastructure are expanding access to cancer care. DICOM-based
harmonization is improving the sharing of computational pathology image-derived data[^2609.14530], while mobile CT   
services integrated with telehealth and regulatory-approved AI aim to bridge diagnostic gaps in remote               
regions[^2609.14347]. Nanoparticle engineering for ovarian cancer theranostics highlights the intersection of        
materials science and oncology[^2609.14720][^2609.14162], and clinical data abstraction tools are being co-designed  
to better support cancer research teams[^2609.19318].                                                                
                                                                                                                     

